---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { SITE, states, statePath } from "../../components/site-data";

const live = states.filter((s) => s.coverage === "live");
const restrictions = states.filter((s) => s.coverage === "restrictions");
const MCP = `${SITE}/mcp`;
const cursorConfig = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { "burn-ban": { url: MCP } } }, null, 2);
const vscodeConfig = JSON.stringify({ servers: { "burn-ban": { type: "http", url: MCP } } }, null, 2);
const genericConfig = JSON.stringify({ mcpServers: { "burn-ban": { type: "http", url: MCP } } }, null, 2);
const alertPrompt = `Every morning at 7, use the burn-ban tools to check list_active_bans for TX and check_burn_ban for ZIP 78701. Only message me if a county I care about is "ban" or "restricted", and include the official link.`;
const sample = JSON.stringify(
  {
    query: { zip: "74103" },
    ambiguous: false,
    approximate: false,
    results: [
      {
        fips: "40143",
        state: "OK",
        county: "Tulsa County",
        status: "none",
        label: "None",
        since: null,
        expires: null,
        sourceUpdated: null,
        checked: "2026-11-23T19:07:00Z",
        sourceName: "Oklahoma Forestry Services",
        sourceUrl: "https://services3.arcgis.com/…/FeatureServer/0",
        officialUrl: "https://experience.arcgis.com/experience/8bdc59b2a54a4383a9a2996fb9407580/",
        url: "/us/ok/tulsa-county/",
      },
    ],
    disclaimer: "Informational only — confirm with the official source.",
  },
  null,
  2,
);
---
<Base
  title="Connect your agent to burn ban status · Is There a Burn Ban?"
  description="Let Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor or any AI agent check US county burn bans and alert you: a free, keyless MCP server and JSON API."
  path="/developers/"
>
  <main id="content" class="page dev">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">Connect your agent</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <h1>Connect your agent</h1>
    <p>
      Your AI assistant can tell you if there's a burn ban where you are, and warn you when one starts.
      Add our MCP server once, then ask in plain words: <q>Is there a burn ban in Tulsa?</q>{" "}It's free and needs no account or key.
    </p>
    <p class="mcp-url"><span>MCP server URL</span> <code>{MCP}</code></p>
    <p class="muted">Setup steps checked against each client's docs on Nov 23, 2026. Menus move; if one doesn't match, look for "custom connector" or "MCP server".</p>

    <h2 id="claude">Claude</h2>
    <p><strong>claude.ai, Claude Desktop, Cowork</strong>: Customize → Connectors → <em>+ Add</em> → <em>Add custom connector</em>. Name it "Burn ban", paste the URL above, and choose <em>No sign in</em>. Free plans allow one custom connector. On Team and Enterprise an owner adds it under Organization settings → Connectors.</p>
    <p><strong>Claude Code</strong>:</p>
    <pre><code>claude mcp add --transport http burn-ban {MCP}</code></pre>
    <p>Add <code>--scope user</code> to use it in every project. Run <code>/mcp</code> to check the connection.</p>

    <h2 id="chatgpt">ChatGPT</h2>
    <p>On the web: Plugins → <em>+</em> → <em>Add custom MCP server</em>. Enter a name and the URL above, set authentication to <em>No authentication</em>, confirm the warning, select <em>Create as a plugin</em>, then install it. Availability depends on your plan and workspace settings.</p>

    <h2 id="editors">Cursor and VS Code</h2>
    <p>Cursor: <code>~/.cursor/mcp.json</code> (all projects) or <code>.cursor/mcp.json</code> (one project).</p>
    <pre><code>{cursorConfig}</code></pre>
    <p>VS Code: <code>.vscode/mcp.json</code>, or run <em>MCP: Add Server</em> and choose HTTP.</p>
    <pre><code>{vscodeConfig}</code></pre>
    <p>Other MCP clients that take JSON (streamable HTTP, stateless, no auth):</p>
    <pre><code>{genericConfig}</code></pre>

    <h2 id="tools">What your agent can do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>check_burn_ban</code>: status for a ZIP, coordinates, county FIPS, or city and state. Returns every county the place touches.</li>
      <li><code>list_active_bans</code>: every county with a ban or restriction right now, optionally for one state.</li>
      <li><code>coverage</code>: which states have live data and how fresh each source is.</li>
      <li><code>subscribe_email_alerts</code>: signs you up for email when a county's status changes. You get a confirmation email and nothing is sent until you click it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="alerts">Get an alert</h2>
    <p>MCP can't push messages to you by itself. Two ways to hear about a new ban:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Ask your agent to check on a schedule.</strong> In an assistant that runs scheduled tasks (for example ChatGPT Tasks, or a Claude Code routine via <code>/schedule</code>), use a prompt like:
        <pre><code>{alertPrompt}</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Subscribe on the site.</strong> Open your county page and tap <em>Alert me</em> for push notifications, or have your agent call <code>subscribe_email_alerts</code> with your email. Alerts go out only when the official source changes.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="http">Plain HTTP, for any agent or script</h2>
    <p>
      A keyless JSON API with open CORS, refreshed about every hour. Add <code>format=text</code> for a one-line answer an agent can quote.
      Please cache for a few minutes and link the official source when you show a status.
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl "{SITE}/api/v1/status?city=Tulsa&amp;state=OK&amp;format=text"
curl "{SITE}/api/v1/status?zip=74103"
curl "{SITE}/api/v1/status?lat=36.154&amp;lon=-95.993"
curl "{SITE}/api/v1/status?fips=40143"
curl "{SITE}/api/v1/bans?state=TX"</code></pre>
    <p>
      Machine-readable: <a href="/openapi.json">OpenAPI spec</a> · <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>.
      Every county and city page also links its JSON with <code>&lt;link rel="alternate" type="application/json"&gt;</code> and a <code>Link</code> header.
    </p>

    <h2>Status values</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><code>ban</code>: the official source reports an active burn ban.</li>
      <li><code>restricted</code>: partial or activity-specific limits (for example an ozone alert or permits required). See <code>label</code>.</li>
      <li><code>none</code>: the source covers this county and reports no ban.</li>
      <li><code>unknown</code>: we can't confirm (no feed, a stale feed, or a feed that only lists bans). Never treat this as "no ban".</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Response</h2>
    <p>
      A ZIP or city can touch more than one county. When it does, <code>ambiguous</code> is <code>true</code> and
      <code>results</code> lists every county, largest share first. City lookups add a <code>place</code> object with the
      city's page. <code>approximate</code> means the ZIP wasn't in Census data and we
      used the nearest one.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{sample}</code></pre>

    <h2>All active bans</h2>
    <pre><code>curl "{SITE}/api/v1/bans"
curl "{SITE}/api/v1/bans?state=TX"</code></pre>
    <p>Returns counties with <code>ban</code> or <code>restricted</code> status, plus the health of each source.</p>

    <h2>Coverage</h2>
    <pre><code>curl "{SITE}/api/v1/coverage"</code></pre>
    <p>Lists every state with its coverage level, publisher and official link, plus source health.</p>

    <h2>States with live data</h2>
    <ul class="inline-list">
      {live.map((s) => <li><a href={statePath(s.abbr)}>{s.name}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
    <p>
      States with restriction feeds (labeled, not county bans): {restrictions.map((s) => s.abbr).join(", ")}.
      Every other state has an official link only. See <a href="/us/">full coverage</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Terms</h2>
    <p>
      Informational only. Burn bans are set by state and local officials; confirm with the official source
      before you burn. Some sources restrict commercial reuse (for example Texas). Check the source's terms
      before you redistribute.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
